// A namespace public that returns std::string& (js::stringify's interop form)
// fed straight to std::format/println. Real std::format formats a string
// reference identically under libstdc++ and libc++ (g++ and clang++ -stdlib
// oracle: both print the JSON). madc classified the arg off the parse-bound
// std::string& overload — rejecting it ("no formatter for pointer type
// basic_string*") or, once unwrapped, taking the ADDRESS of a call that in
// fact lowers to the lean const char* form. The fix classifies a call arg off
// its CIR-resolved callee's return type (what translate_expr lowers through),
// so the classification and the lowering agree and the result is flavor-
// invariant.
var v = { "k": 42, "n": "hi" };
std::string buf;
println("[{}]", js::stringify(buf, v));
